es spends  almost six days stacking their car 150  hours costs our economy five billion  pounds what i've published today is an  independent report how we get to net  zero by 20 for a wake-up call to the  government to give us more support to  tackle these triple challenges but also  i want to take londoners with us it's a  conversation i'm starting with londoners  about how we reduce road traffic over  the course of the next eight years one  of the things i'm quite clear about is  the poorest londoners least likely to  own a car who suffer the worst  consequences from air pollution  congestion and the climate emergency i'm  going to try and encourage londoners  working together how we can have over  the course of the next eight or nine  years about a quarter 27 fewer journeys  in their cars a number of things we've  got to do  make it safer for people to cycle  encourage people to walk and make public  transport an attractive alternative  coming up here on the daily climate show  we'll be talking a bit more about  congestion charging in cities in just a  moment with the director of policy and  translation at the grantham institute  elissa gilbert and professor of global  change science at university college  london and the university of leeds simon  lewis  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news and we're  getting straight to discussing the  climate issues of the day now with  director of policy and translation at  the grantham institute alyssa gilbert a  professor of global change science at  university college london and university  of leeds simon lewis so welcome to both  of you nice to see you both  so a war on motorists or a good day for  london's kids sadiq khan's plans to  clean up london's air by charging people  for using their cars have received some  mixed reviews the london mayor says air  pollution and the climate emergency are  an issue of social justice across the  globe and the poorest and least likely  to own a car are suffering the  consequences so alyssa what do you think  charging people more to drive their cars  is that a good idea as far as the  planet's concerned  well i think it's it's not quite as  stark as you put it it's an extension of  what we call the polluter pays principle  really we want people to have to pay a  little bit more who are doing more  damage to our environment and in fact  people's health so i think what the  mayor is suggesting is a more complex  structure of paying to use roads than we  have at the moment in london we've got  the congestion charge and you pay if you  use public transport but it's a bit  blunt really so i think what we're  hearing is this idea of a more  sophisticated set of charging  instruments that really reflects the  amount of pollution that you might be  causing well yes and one of the ideas  isn't it simon is to get people to use  cleaner vehicles if they are going to  drive but the criticism is i think the  rac makes it is that cleaner vehicles  are just too expensive for most people  electric vehicles cost more to buy don't  they  they do a present but what we  really  should be doing is looking at the  alternatives making the clean green  choice  walking cycling electric bikes public  transport the sensible option and the  last option of those is is an electric  vehicle or a conventional vehicle and  that's where the focus on the pricing  goes too far because we really need to  focus on the infrastructure to make  cycling safe  on making public transport as accessible  as possible and then that can work for  londoners because those are often  cheaper choices than  using a car and then we'll be able to  drive people away from this mode of  transport that's contributing air  pollution and also death from road  traffic accidents to move away from that  and then we'll have more  freeroads free from congestion for the  actual professional drivers who need it  the paramedics that need to get to  emergencies the plumbers who need to get  