to people's houses with their tools and  then we'll have a much greener more  livable city alyssa do you think that  would work and there are huge challenges  with that aren't they making public  transport cheaper is going to be very  expensive some people are scared of  cycling in cities because of all the  vehicles around and also people like the  individual freedom of thinking i want to  get in my car and go where i want to go  well i think we shouldn't overestimate  the amount of change that this is for  london london is a fantastic city in  terms of transport options we have all  kinds of buses tubes um trams we have  you can even travel on that sky car  across london at one point  um we've got a riverboat we're using all  modes of transport in london we've got  parts of london that are extremely  walkable with local services close by so  we're not thinking about transforming  something from one state car only to  something completely different it's  really just  a gentle  nudging almost to demonstrate to people  that they've got these other options in  front of them and to use them and it  won't be appropriate for everyone all  the time that's not what this is about  but i do think it's very possible  especially in a city like london well  yes and one of the things being  considered simon i think is this idea of  charging people who are outside of a  greater london zone to come into it  isn't that likely to hit those people  who can't afford to live in central  london people who are paid less like  carers teachers nhs staff potentially  and they're going to get penalised for  travelling in well i think these plans  have got to be very carefully uh  considered to make sure that they are  not impacting the poorest members of  society  but again we  encouraging want to encourage people  onto public transport from outer london  to get into central london and that's  making  those public transport services as  affordable as possible so they're more  attractive than than taking your car  and alyssa how do you get people on side  to plans like this  well i think  it's it's not by a sort of big scary  newspaper headline it's obviously by  engaging with people speaking to them  proper consultation and really listening  to their concerns and really hearing  them so there will be some people with  genuine concerns you just mentioned some  groups of people who will be concerned  about how they travel  how do they feel like they're traveling  safely how can they make the most of  other travel or transport opportunities  people perhaps with concerns about their  businesses but they'll also be people  there who will genuinely see the  opportunity for gain so i think it's  making an amount of time for proper  engagement and consultation with a wider  group of people in the city and that's a  really important part of planning the  policy and then i think also leaving  space to test the policy and then  allowing a little bit of tweaking to  make it work um work well and work  better  okay let's move on to our second topic  shall we because investment fund  managers aren't generally household  names but if you are gonna know one it  would probably be larry fink chief  executive of blackrock now in his latest  annual letter to ceos he said that  pushing climate policies was about  profits not being woke so what  difference can an intervention like this  actually make so simon larry fink says  that they focus on sustainability not  because they're environmentalists but  because they're capitalists so  do interventions like this make any real  difference to corporate behaviour do you  think  i think it is an important intervention  although  when the media portray things as woke  that's usually about not having a  conversation about something rather than  having a meaningful conversation about  it and what larry fink is saying is that  even from his very narrowest perspective  of just making more money  it makes sense to drive down emissions  as fast as possible to get to net zero  and push for the policies to do that  from that narrow perspective and that's  really important and it's important for  him as a leader in the investment  community and it's important for leaders  in all aspects of society to speak up  about the need to stabilize the climate  system and that's what's important here  and what larry fink is saying but where  i differ from him  is that i think this energy transition  we can go through this energy transition  not just by  being net zero consumers but by  allowing people to to have a better  lives rather than just enriching the  billionaire class that larry fink  is part of  well yes and alyssa there is a suspicion  when any big businessman speaks out  isn't there there's always a worry that  there's some green washing going on  somewhere among some companies and i  know that one  ben cushing from the sierra club  example has said there's not much to see  here other than more hot air from a  would-be climate leader do you think  that's a fair criticism  well i think really it's a message that  he's giving um to inspire other people  around him to act uh so  i i that seems a little bit unfair to me  it's of course part of a longer message  that he sent out to investors as a  leader in that community  where he talks about a lot of other  things as well as acting sustainably and  i think it's fantastic i think to see  the sustainability message in there and  what could otherwise be just a very  strong invest carefully invest wisely  think about the long-term  kind of message that he's sending to  those people who really trust him and  hear his voice um so  he doesn't need to see it at all  actually um so i think we should  recognize it as a really positive  message sent  within his community and simon do you  think that persuading big corporations  to make green choices is is more  important than governments making green  policies are are the two things  inextricably linked  it's what's important is  the government because they set the  regulatory framework they set the laws  within which these corporations operate  so get those laws right to drive down  emissions to near zero and the companies  will have to follow but of course  companies are incredibly important in  terms of their power and influence so in  some senses they are inextricably linked  but one thing  that governments have is that they their  populations can hold them to account  so that they can drive this zero project  forward to stabilize the climate system  and your line just about held up there  but alyssa a final thought from you  business leaders political leaders  celebrities which are the voices you  most welcome speaking out on this issue  what do you think makes the most  difference as far as the public is  concerned  the unexpected voices there are a lot of  people out there that  the the public are expecting to hear say  these things but when you hear it come  from left field a really different voice  that's really important because the  people who usually trust that person on  other issues are going to hear something  new for the first time and think about  it differently than if they hear it from  the usual suspects okay well alyssa  gilbert and simon lewis always  interesting to get your take on things  thanks very much indeed  and tomorrow i'm going to be joined by  the chief executive of climate group  helen clarkson and environmentalist and  columnist for the guardian george monbio  that's it for now thanks for watching  see you next time  [Music]
 

